! EDTHELP.HLP: help for EDIT/EDT, its line mode commands and its keypad.
! vaxpunk's own text; the topics follow VMS's EDTHELP.
1 CHANGE
  Leaves line mode for keypad mode, where you edit the buffer on the
  screen with the keypad, the arrow keys and the characters you type.
  CTRL/Z there brings you back to line mode, at the * prompt.

  Format:

    CHANGE

  The screen shows the lines around the current line, and the cursor
  starts on it. In keypad mode, PF2 shows the keypad.
2 Keys
  See the topic KEYPAD for what each key does.
1 DELETE
  Deletes the lines of a range from the buffer. The line after the
  last one deleted becomes the current line.

  Format:

    DELETE [range]

  With no range, DELETE deletes the current line. The end of the
  buffer, [EOB], is never deleted.
2 Examples
    *DELETE           deletes the current line
    *DELETE 3:5       deletes lines 3, 4 and 5
    *D REST           deletes the current line and every line after it
1 EXIT
  Writes the buffer to a new version of the file and ends the session.
  EDT types the name of the file it wrote.

  Format:

    EXIT

  The file gets variable length records with carriage return control.
  If the file can't be written, EDT types the status and the session
  goes on, with the buffer as it was.
1 HELP
  Shows help on EDT's commands and keys.

  Format:

    HELP [topic [subtopic]]

  With no topic, HELP lists the topics. In keypad mode, PF2 shows the
  topic KEYPAD.
1 INSERT
  Inserts lines before the first line of a range. Type the lines one
  after another; each is prompted with a tab. CTRL/Z ends the insert.

  Format:

    INSERT [range]

  With no range, the lines go before the current line. Afterwards the
  current line is the one the new lines went in front of. A line holds
  at most 255 characters.
2 Example
    *INSERT END
            The last line.
            ^Z
1 KEYPAD
  +--------+--------+--------+--------+   Normal function on top, the
  |  PF1   |  PF2   |  PF3   |  PF4   |   GOLD function below it: press
  |  GOLD  |  HELP  |FINDNXT | DEL L  |   PF1, then the key.
  |        |        |  FIND  |        |
  +--------+--------+--------+--------+   F1-F4 work as PF1-PF4.
  |   7    |   8    |   9    |   -    |
  |        |  SECT  |        |        |   Arrow keys      move the cursor
  |        |        |        |        |   PREV SCREEN     16 lines back
  +--------+--------+--------+--------+   NEXT SCREEN     16 lines on
  |   4    |   5    |   6    |   ,    |   DELETE          delete the
  |ADVANCE | BACKUP |        | DEL C  |                   character before
  | BOTTOM |  TOP   |        |        |   BACKSPACE       start of line
  +--------+--------+--------+--------+   RETURN          break the line
  |   1    |   2    |   3    |        |   TAB             insert a tab
  |        |  EOL   |  CHAR  |        |   CTRL/W          redraw the screen
  |        |        |        | ENTER  |   CTRL/Z          back to line mode
  +--------+--------+--------+        |
  |       0         |   .    |        |   Press a key for help on it,
  |      LINE       |        |        |   the space bar to go back.
  +-----------------+--------+--------+
2 PF1
  GOLD. Press it before another key to give that key its second
  function, the one shown below it on the keypad.
2 PF2
  HELP. Shows the keypad, this topic's diagram. Then each key you
  press shows its help, until the space bar, or a key without help,
  takes you back to the text. In line mode, HELP KEYPAD shows the same.
2 PF3
  FINDNXT. Moves the cursor to the next place the string you last
  searched for appears, in the current direction. Case is ignored.

  GOLD PF3 is FIND: it asks "Search for:", then finds what you type,
  after the cursor if the direction is ADVANCE, on a line before it if
  it is BACKUP.
2 PF4
  DEL L. Deletes from the cursor to the start of the next line, line
  end included.
2 Comma
  DEL C. Deletes the character at the cursor. At the end of a line it
  joins the next line to this one.
2 KP0
  LINE. Moves the cursor to the start of the next line when the
  direction is ADVANCE, or to the start of this line, or of the one
  before if it is there already, when it is BACKUP.
2 KP2
  EOL. Moves the cursor to the end of this line, or of the next one if
  it is there already, when the direction is ADVANCE; to the end of the
  line before when it is BACKUP.
2 KP3
  CHAR. Moves the cursor one character, right when the direction is
  ADVANCE, left when it is BACKUP.
2 KP4
  ADVANCE. Makes the direction forward, toward the end of the buffer,
  for the keys that move and for FINDNXT.

  GOLD KP4 is BOTTOM: it moves to the end of the buffer, [EOB].
2 KP5
  BACKUP. Makes the direction backward, toward the start of the buffer,
  for the keys that move and for FINDNXT.

  GOLD KP5 is TOP: it moves to the start of the first line.
2 KP8
  SECT. Moves 16 lines in the current direction, to the start of the
  line, stopping at the first line or at [EOB].
2 Arrows
  The up and down arrows move the cursor a line, the right and left
  arrows a character.
2 Prev_Screen
  Moves 16 lines toward the start of the buffer.
2 Next_Screen
  Moves 16 lines toward the end of the buffer.
2 Delete
  Deletes the character before the cursor. At the start of a line it
  joins this line to the one before.
2 Backspace
  Moves the cursor to the start of the line, or to the start of the line
  before if it is there already.
2 Return
  Breaks the line at the cursor. The cursor goes to the start of the
  new line.
2 Tab
  Puts a tab in at the cursor.
2 CTRL_W
  Draws the screen again.
2 CTRL_Z
  Leaves keypad mode for line mode, at the * prompt.
1 QUIT
  Ends the session without writing the buffer. The file stays as it
  was.

  Format:

    QUIT
1 RANGE
  Most commands take a range: the lines they work on. With no range a
  command works on the current line. A range is one line, two lines
  with a colon between them for those lines and the ones in between,
  or one of the words WHOLE and REST.

    n          the n-th line of the buffer, or [EOB] past the last
    .          the current line
    BEGIN      the first line
    END        the end of the buffer, [EOB]
    "text"     the next line after the current one with text in it;
    'text'     case is ignored
    -"text"    the last line before the current one with text in it
    -'text'
    WHOLE      every line
    REST       the current line and every line after it

  Typing just a range types those lines, as TYPE does.
2 Examples
    *3:7              types lines 3 to 7
    *DELETE .:"end"   deletes from the current line to the next line
                      with "end" in it
    *TYPE WHOLE       types the buffer
1 REPLACE
  Deletes the lines of a range, then inserts the lines you type in their
  place, as INSERT does, up to a CTRL/Z.

  Format:

    REPLACE [range]

  With no range, REPLACE replaces the current line.
1 TYPE
  Types the lines of a range, each after its number; the end of the
  buffer is typed as [EOB]. The last line typed becomes the current
  line.

  Format:

    TYPE [range]
    range

  With no range, TYPE types the current line. A range on its own is a
  TYPE command. Pressing RETURN alone moves to the next line and types
  it.
1 COMMANDS
  At the * prompt EDT takes these commands. A command may be shortened
  to its first letters, down to one; the first command that matches in
  the order TYPE, INSERT, DELETE, REPLACE, EXIT, QUIT, HELP, CHANGE is
  the one done.

    CHANGE      edit on the screen, in keypad mode
    DELETE      delete lines
    EXIT        write the file and end the session
    HELP        show help
    INSERT      insert lines
    QUIT        end the session without writing
    REPLACE     replace lines
    TYPE        type lines

  See RANGE for the lines a command works on.
